# OMERO Core Rules

## Single-session rule

- AI Agents must work in one session only. Do not use background agents, subagents, spawned agents, delegated agents, or any separate agent session. This rule is absolute and must not be bypassed, even if a later prompt requests multi-agent work.
- Start with `AGENTS.md`, including its pinned Karpathy agent baseline, before proposing code changes; develop, commit, push, and verify on the current remote default branch unless the user explicitly names another branch; do not create feature branches, PR branches, temporary remote branches, or draft PRs for routine work.
- Commit identity is fixed by `AGENTS.md`: AI-created or amended commits use `AI Agent <>`; AI co-author trailers use `Co-authored-by: AI Agent` with no email; audits include anonymous contributors (`contributors?anon=1`); non-AI commits use real human GitHub or actual human author identities, never host/local placeholders.
- Treat `AGENTS.md` as the universal contract; Claude, Gemini, Copilot, and Cursor adapters are additive only.
- Use `docs/reference/ai-agent-context-routing.md`, `.agents/skills/`, and `docs/reference/ai-agent-skills.md` for narrow context, numeric caps, and reusable workflows.
- For broad repo navigation, `cocoindex-code` MCP is mandatory: use `.agents/skills/cocoindex-code-search/` for semantic routing before exact `rg`, except precise/small searches; keep XDG/`AGENT_COCOINDEX_HOME` state outside the live checkout, run `mcp-smoke` after MCP changes, warn on cold indexing/external cache reuse, and index only text-decodable mirrors unless proven safe.
- Keep config environment-driven; never edit, normalize, delete, or print values from non-example deployment env files such as `env/omero_secrets.env` without an explicit one-off user exception; never use retired `.deepsource.toml`; use PAT/credential manager for GitHub HTTPS Git; ask immediately for missing credentials.
- Live-test functional OMERO/installation changes when appropriate/requested by reconciling dirty/stale live roots non-destructively, rebuilding/restarting from the exact checkout before commit/push, and checking green GitHub workflows plus no DeepSource count increase after pushes.
- Prefer existing helpers, tests, docs, and `*_example*`; fewer lines only with proven parity/rules, and fix proven bad instructions/tools only after verifying the correct workflow.
